## 1. Introduction
$17\upbeta$ -Estradiol (E2), a major sex hormone, is one of the most active mammalian estrogen steroids [1,2]. In recent years, E2 was shown to have severe negative impact on the endocrine system of humans and aquatic organisms as a result of its abuse in contraception, clinical treatment [3], animal fattening [4,5], etc. Intake of E2 by human beings for a prolonged period of time through food chains or other ways, even in extremely low concentrations leads to multifarious and serious consequences, such as abnormal growth, male infertility, and breast cancer [6]. Hence, $17\upbeta$ -Estradiol has become a significantly harmful endocrine disrupting compound [7,8]. Therefore, development of an effective and sensitive method to detect E2 in biological samples is important for monitoring the health of humans and animals [9]. Till date, researchers have developed numerous methods for detecting E2, for instance, surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) [10], colorimetry [11], electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) [12–14], and high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) [15].
Fluorescence analysis is widely used for detection of ions [16–18], molecules [19,20], biomarkers [21–23], DNA [24] or RNA [25], and tumor cells [22,26], due to its advantages such as simplicity, speed, and sensitivity [27]. Analytical conditions and background signals can interfere with single signal fluorescence detection, resulting in inaccurate results [28,29]. In order to solve these problems, a ratio fluorescence sensor, provided with selfcalibration function, which can correct the interference of external factors, has been developed in recent years to increase the accuracy [30–32]. When the emission spectrum of a fluorophore (donor) overlaps with the absorption spectrum of a quencher (acceptor), the transfer of fluorescence energy from the donor to the acceptor, termed as fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET), can be observed [33,34]. Furthermore, the mechanism of FRET depends on the distance between the donor and the acceptor [35]. Fluorescence resonance energy transfer mechanism and ratio detection are often used together to improve the sensitivity and accuracy of the sensor [36,37]. For example, Zhang et al. synthesized an aptamer-conjugated nanocomplex as a self-standard FRET sensor for quantitative detection of exosomes [38].
Aptamers are widely used in the assembly of biosensors, owing to their high specificity, good stability, and easy functionalization [39]. Generally, the key to construct a FRET sensor is to find a suitable pair of fluorescence donor and acceptor. Nanomaterials are widely used in the self-assembly of sensors, due to their unique characteristics, such as quantum size effect, large specific surface area, and excellent optical, electrical, and magnetic properties. So far, sensors based on nanomaterials have been successfully used for the detection of related analytes in food, environment, and medicine [40–42]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been extensively used for constructing fluorescence sensing platforms owing to their long excited states lifetime, easy modification, and high porosity [43,44]. Therefore, many MOFs-based biosensors were constructed for sensitive detection of multifarious biomarkers [45]. For instance, $M\mathrm{IL}{-}53{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ (blue-emission) and quantum dots (green-emission) were used for fabricating a ratio fluorescence sensor to selectively detect 6-mercaptopurine [46]. Ru $(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ is one of the most extensively researched molecules used for the construction of optical sensors, due to its excellent luminescence, high chemical stability, and good solubility in aqueous and organic solutions [47]. However, the good solubility of $\mathtt{R u}(\mathtt{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ limits its direct applications in the assembly of sensors. Hence, the immobilization of $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathsf{b p y}})_{3}^{2+}$ is crucial for improving its luminescence properties [32]. Recently, $\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ -doped silica nanoparticles $\mathrm{\left(RuSiO_{2}\right)}$ ) have gained the attention of researchers owing to their stable chemical properties, convenient modification, and excellent biocompatibility [48]. For example, Chen et al. used CdTe quantum dots to build an electrochemiluminescence resonance energy transfer platform inside $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ nanospheres for sensitive detection of $\mathsf{\Omega}\propto$ -Ergocryptine [49].
In general, a FRET-based ratiometric sensing platform was designed for the quantitative estimation of $17\upbeta$ -Estradiol. Specifically, MIL- $53\ –\ensuremath{\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ was synthesized by a hydrothermal method and covalently linked with streptavidin. It was further linked to a biotin modified E2 aptamer through avidin-biotin specific binding forming MIL-53-apt, which acted as the FRET donor. $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles were prepared by reverse microemulsion method and covalently linked to the complementary aptamers after amination. The spacing between MIL-53-apt and $\mathsf{R u S i O}_{2}$ -cDNA was shortened when the two fluorescence probes were combined by complementary base pairing. This resulted in FRET, because of the large overlapping of the absorption spectrum of acceptor with the emission spectrum of donor. In presence of E2, the E2 aptamer and E2 have higher specificity than the complementary aptamer. This causes the FRET process to cut-off, resulting in the fluorescence emission recovery of donor and decrease in fluorescence emission of acceptor, thus further realizing the detection of E2.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Reagents and materials
2-Aminoterephtalic acid $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ -BDC, $99\%$ ) and bovine serum albumin (BSA, $99\%$ were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich, Tetraethyl orthosilicate $(\mathsf{S i O}_{2}~\ge~28.4\%)$ and ${\sf A l C l}_{3}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ $(99\%)$ were bought from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. Tris( $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -Bipyri dyl)-dichlororuthenium(II) hexahydrate $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}\mathsf{C l}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $98\%$ , triton X-100 $(99\%)$ , $n$ -hexanol, cyclohexane, (3-aminopropyl) triethoxysilane (APTES, $98\%$ and glutaraldehyde (GA, $25\%$ were procured from Adamas Reagent Ltd. The E2 aptamer sequence $5^{\prime}$ -GCT-TCC-AGCTTA-TTG-AAT-TAC-ACG-CAG-AGG-GTA-GCG-GC T-CTG-CGC-ATTCAA-TTG-CTG-CGC-GCT-GAA-GCG-CGG-AAG-C-Bi otin- ${\cdot}3^{\prime}$ ) and complementary DNA (cDNA, $5^{\prime}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ - $(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{6}$ -GCT-TCCGCG-CTT-CAG-CGC-GCA-GCA-A- $3^{\prime}$ ), TE buffer ( $10\mathrm{\mM}$ Tris-HCl; $1\mathrm{mM}$ EDTA, $\mathrm{pH}=8.0\dot{}$ , and goat serum were procured from Shanghai Sangon Biotechnology Co. Ltd. TE buffers with different pH values were prepared by adjusting the pH with hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide solutions. Phosphate buffer solution (PBS) $10~\mathrm{mM}$ , $\mathsf{p H}=7.4$ was prepared by mixing ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf H P O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N a H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{4}$ according to specific proportions. All chemicals used in this work were of analytical reagent grade. Deionized water was used for all the experiments.
## 2.2. Experimental instruments
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was conducted on a JEOL JEM-2010F electron microscope, operating at $200~\mathrm{kV}.$ Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was conducted on Hitachi S4800. Energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectra were acquired on a scanning electron microscope, operating at $15{\mathrm{kV}}.$ . X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were acquired using a Bruker D8 ADVANCE diffractometer, employing Cu Ka radiation source ( $40\ \mathrm{\mA}$ and $40~\mathrm{kV},$ . The fluorescence spectra were recorded on an Edinburgh FLS920 spectrophotometer using 450 W xenon lamp as the excitation light source with an appropriate cutoff filter. The UV–Vis absorption spectra were recorded using a Shimadzu spectrophotometer. Fourier transform infrared spectra were acquired on a Nicolet IS10 infrared spectrophotometer. The Zeta potentials were measured using an Anton Paar Litesizer 500.
## 2.3. Synthesis of MIL-53-apt
MIL-53- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was synthesized according to a previous reference, but with slight modification [50]. First, ${\sf A l C l}_{3}{\cdot}6{\sf H}_{2}0$ $\cdot493.6~\mathrm{mg}^{\cdot}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC $375.6\mathrm{mg}^{\cdot}$ were mixed in $5\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water by ultrasonication. The mixture was transferred to a $25~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined autoclave and reacted at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The yellow product was obtained after centrifugation and washing thrice with deionized water. Thereafter, the product was immersed in DMF and maintained at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ to remove any unreacted $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC. Subsequently, the DMF-treated product was placed in methanol solution overnight for exchanging with DMF present in pores of MOFs and then dried in an oven at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ . Following this, as-obtained MIL$53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ $(5~\mathrm{mg})$ was dispersed in $1~\mathrm{mL}$ PBS containing $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ GA and magnetically stirred for $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ . The product, cross-linked with the amino-group of streptavidin $(50~\upmu\mathrm{L},\:1~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ , was collected after centrifugation. Further, the E2 aptamer $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{M}})$ was linked to the streptavidin-conjugated MIL- $53\ –\ensuremath{\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ through avidin-biotin specific binding. After centrifugal washing, $2\%$ BSA was used to block the nonspecific binding sites. Ultimately, the as-synthesized MIL-53-apt was transferred to $\mathrm{pH}=8.0$ TE buffer and maintained at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for subsequent experiments.
## 2.4. Synthesis of $R u S i O_{2}$ -cDNA
$\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles were synthesized by the reverse microemulsion method [51]. Then, the as-prepared $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ $(20~\mathrm{mg})$ was added into $5~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol solution containing $400~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ APTES and stirred for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ at room temperature. After several times of centrifugal washing with ethanol, amino group functionalized $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles $\left(\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}–\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ ) were obtained after drying in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}C$ . $\mathsf{R u S i O}_{2}–\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ $(5~\mathrm{mg})$ was dispersed in PBS $\mathrm{1~mL}$ containing GA ${\bf\rho}_{\mathrm{i00~\upmuL}})$ and linked to the amino group of cDNA $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $100\upmu\mathrm{M})$ by cross-linking. The as-prepared $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ - cDNA was redistributed in $\mathrm{pH}=8.0$ TE buffer and stored at $4^{\circ}C$ for further use.
## 2.5. Fluorescence aptasensor for determination of E2
The assembly and detection mechanism of the FRET aptasensor has been illustrated in Scheme 1. Specifically, MIL-53-apt $(20~\upmu\mathrm{L})$
was mixed with $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA $(60~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ in $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ to obtain a hybrid complex solution. Then, different concentrations of E2 $(120~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were added into the above solution. After incubation for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ fluorescence signals of the mixtures under the excitation wavelength of $329~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were measured using Edinburgh FLS920 spectrophotometer.
## 2.6. Real sample preparation
Serum sample was prepared by ultrasonically mixing goat serum $(0.5~\mathrm{mL})$ with acetonitrile $\mathrm{:0.5~mL}$ for $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The mixture was centrifuged $(10,000~\mathrm{rpm})$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , and the supernatant was diluted by $\mathrm{pH}=8.0$ TE buffer in a ratio of 1:10 and used for subsequent experiments.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterizations of MIL-53-apt and $R u S i O_{2}$ -cDNA
To confirm the successful syntheses of two fluorescent probes, several characterization methods were employed in this work. The SEM image of MIL- $.53\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ in Fig. 1A displayed regular rhomboid structures of about $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ sizes. In Fig. 1B, the PXRD pattern of MIL- $53\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH_{2}}}$ was consistent with the simulated one, indicating the successful preparation of MIL- $.53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . The FTIR spectrum of MIL- $.53\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ is shown in Fig. S1. Two sharp peaks at $3390~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3502~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the symmetric and asymmetric stretching vibrations of the $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ group, respectively. A series of peaks from $1400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ to $1700\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be ascribed to the carbonyl vibration stretching vibrations of DMF, which remained in the pores of MIL- $.53\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . From Fig. S2, it was evident that the characteristic elements of MIL- $.53\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH_{2}}}$ included C, O, and Al. Furthermore, the EDX results of MIL-53-apt in Fig. S3 testified that the E2 aptamer was effectively modified on MIL- $.53\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH_{2}}}$ (the additional elements, P and S, were due to E2 aptamers and streptavidin, respectively). MIL-53-apt showed hardly any changes, but its particle size increased to about to $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ after modification with streptavidin and E2 aptamer, as shown in Fig. S4. Moreover, in Fig. S5, the UV–visible spectrum of MIL-53-apt showed blue-shift compared with the MIL- $.53\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ , which was probably due to the successful connection of the E2 aptamer with the MIL- $.53\ –\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ through specific bonding between biotin and avidin. Meanwhile, the fluorescence spectrum of MIL-53-apt blue-shifted from $463~\mathrm{{nm}}$ to $428\mathrm{nm}$ in Fig. 2A. It could also be seen from Fig. S6 that the E2 aptamer carried negative charge, whereas streptavidin and $M\mathrm{IL}{-}53{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ carried positive charges. After modification of MIL-53- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ with streptavidin, the zeta potential of MIL-53-streptavidin composites increased to $21~\mathrm{mV}$ . When the composite was linked with E2 aptamer, the zeta potential of MIL-53-apt dropped to $11.6~\mathrm{mV}$ . The above results testified the successful preparation of MIL-53-apt.
The as-obtained $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles showed typical spherical morphology with diameters of $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ in Fig. 1C. The elemental analysis of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ based on the EDX spectrum is shown in
Fig. 1D. The major elements were found to be C, N, O, Si, and Ru, indicating the successful preparation of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. S7, the $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA was still spherical in shape but the diameter increased to $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ . This proved that cDNA was successfully attached to the surface of the $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ without causing aggregation of nanoparticles. Further, the EDX spectrum in Fig. S8 proved the effective synthesis of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA (the extra P element was attributed to cDNA). Moreover, the UV–visible absorption spectrum of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ showed three characteristic peaks at $242~\mathrm{{nm}}$ $286\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and $455\ \mathrm{nm}$ , consistent with the $\operatorname{Ru}({\mathsf{b p y}})_{3}^{2+}$ absorption spectrum in Fig. S9. After amination of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ , the UV–visible absorption spectrum of ${\mathsf{R u S i O}}_{2}–{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ showed hardly any changes. However, when cDNA was crosslinked with ${\mathsf{R u S i O}}_{2}–{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ through
GA, the absorption peak at $242~\mathrm{{nm}}$ for $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA almost disappeared. Accordingly, the fluorescence spectrum of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA was slightly blue-shifted from $612~\mathrm{{nm}}$ to $608~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , as displayed in Fig. 2A. In Fig. S10, the cDNA and $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ carried negative charges. After amination, the zeta potential of ${\mathsf{R u S i O}}_{2}–{\mathsf{N H}}_{2}$ increased to $-4.4~\mathrm{mV}$ . After linking with cDNA, the zeta potential of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ - cDNA decreased to $-7.7\ \mathrm{mV}$ . The above results testified the successful synthesis of ${\mathrm{RuSiO}}_{2}$ -cDNA. With same mass concentration, from Fig. 2B, the fluorescence intensity of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ was 55 times that of $\mathtt{R u}(\mathtt{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ , suggesting that the encapsulation of $\mathtt{R u}(\mathtt{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ inside the silica could effectively improve the fluorescence intensity of Ru $(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ . This could be due to the increase in number of $\mathtt{R u}(\mathtt{b p y})_{3}^{2+}$ molecules doped per nanoparticle.
## 3.2. Investigation of fluorescence resonance energy transfer mechanism
A ratiometric fluorescence sensor was proposed for the quantitative estimation of E2 during the FRET process between MIL-53- apt and $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA. Following experiments were conducted to explore the mechanism of fluorescence resonance energy transfer mechanism. When the donor and the acceptor were combined by base complementary pairing, the distance between them was reduced. As shown in Fig. S11, the distance between the two probes after hybridization met the requirements of fluorescence resonance energy transfer. It can be seen from Fig. 3A that the UV–visible absorption peaks of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA (curve a) and MIL-53-apt (curve b) do not interfere with each other. However, a large overlapping region between the fluorescence emission of MIL-53-apt and the UV–visible absorption of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA was clearly seen in Fig. 3B, proving the feasibility of fluorescence resonance energy transfer. Therefore, from the curve c of Fig. 3C, it was evident that after hybridization of MIL-53-apt and $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA, the fluorescence intensity of MIL-53-apt was reduced $\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=428-$ nm), whereas the fluorescence intensity of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA was enhanced ${\langle\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=608\ \mathrm{nm}}$ ). This further demonstrated the feasibility of the fluorescence resonance energy transfer mechanism. The fluorescent color changes of different solutions were observed by the naked eye using a $310\mathrm{nm}$ UV lamp (Fig. 3C inset). Specifically, the fluorescence emissions of MIL-53-apt (a) and $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA (b) showed blue and orange colors, respectively, whereas the fluorescence emission turned pink (c) after the two probes hybridized. The relevant CIE coordinates from a to b were (0.152, 0.067), (0.607, 0.392), and (0.299, 0.176) separately in Fig. 3D.
## 3.3. Optimization of experimental conditions
The optimal conditions of the key parameters affecting the sensor performance were explored. With a certain mass concentration of MIL-53-apt, the effect of mass concentration of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA on the fluorescence intensity was studied. It could be seen from Fig. S12 that as the mass ratio of MIL-53-apt to $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA changed from 2:1 to 1:3, the fluorescence intensity of MIL-53-apt decreased gradually and the fluorescence intensity of ${\mathrm{RuSiO}}_{2}.$ - cDNA increased constantly. When the ratio reached 1:3, the fluorescence intensity of MIL-53-apt remained stable, indicating that the combination of the two probes reached saturation. Therefore, the ratio was fixed at 1:3 in the follow-up experiments. Next, the time required for hybridization between MIL-53-apt and $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA was researched. As illustrated in Fig. S13, the fluorescence intensity ratio at $428\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $608\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\left(\mathrm{I}_{428}/\mathrm{I}_{608}\right)$ changed with increase in hybridization time. At $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , the ratio reached its maximum, and so the hybridization time was maintained as $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ for the subsequent experiments. Moreover, the influence of incubation time between E2 and the E2 aptamer on $\mathrm{I}_{428}/\mathrm{I}_{608}$ was explored. With increase in incubation time, the ratio increased continuously until it reached maximum at $^\mathrm{~2~h~}$ , as seen in Fig. S14. The effect of pH on $\mathrm{I}_{428}/\mathrm{I}_{608}$ was also studied by adjusting the pH value of TE buffer. Fig. S15 showed that the fluorescence ratio was almost same in acidic and neutral conditions, but it increased significantly in alkaline conditions. Therefore, $\mathsf{p H}=8.0$ TE buffer was chosen for the subsequent experiments.
## 3.4. Quantitative estimation of E2
Firstly, the hybridization of MIL-53-apt and $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA was through complementary base pairing. This shortened the distance between the two probes, leading to resonance energy transfer. Subsequently, when different concentrations of E2 were present in the system, the strong specific recognition between E2 aptamer and E2 destroyed the energy transfer process to varying extent. This caused the recovery of fluorescence of MIL-53-apt and the fluorescence of $\mathrm{RuSiO}_{2}$ -cDNA was quenched, as shown in Fig. 4A. The calibration curve with a satisfying linear correlation coefficient $({\mathrm R}^{2}=0.99573)$ was acquired between the fluorescence intensity ratio $\left(\mathrm{I}_{428}/\mathrm{I}_{608}\right)$ and concentration logarithm of E2, in the range between 0.5 and $1000\ \mathrm{nM}$ . Moreover, the corresponding linear regression equation of the sensor was $\mathrm{I}_{428}/\mathrm{I}_{608}=1.29\mathrm{~\lg~~C~}$ $\left(\mathsf{E}2\right)+\mathsf{1}.99$ , with its detection limit (LOD) of $0.2\ \mathrm{nM}$ in Fig. 4B. LOD was calculated according to the definition by IUPAC $\mathrm{\hat{C}_{D L}}=3$ $S_{\mathrm{b}}\mathrm{m}^{-1}.$ ), where m is the slope of the calibration curve and $S_{\mathrm{b}}$ is the standard deviation of 20 blank solutions. It can be seen from Table S1 that compared with other biosensors from previous reports, our proposed sensing strategy based on FRET, showed better sensitivity for detection of E2.
## 3.5. Sensing performance of the aptasensor
Some properties of the biosensor, which can affect its application in real samples, such as selectivity and reproducibility, have been explored in Fig. 5. Considering the possible interference of substances in serum, we selected the following substances for selective experiments [52]. Herein, NaCl, $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{M g C l}_{2}$ , L-histidine, urea, glucose, estriol, progesterone, and their mixture (all interfering substances with E2) were selected to replace the detection target E2 for fluorescence detection in Fig. 5A. The experimental results showed that the presence of other species in the system had little effect on the detection of E2, suggesting high selectivity of the sensor for $17\upbeta$ -Estradiol. Moreover, three times repeated detection of different E2 concentrations, with relative standard deviation (RSD) from $1.5\%$ to $4.1\%$ , as shown in Fig. 5B, indicated that the sensor had satisfactory reproducibility for the determination of E2.
## 3.6. Determination E2 in actual sample
It is evident from the above experimental results that the proposed sensor has high selectivity and great reproducibility. Therefore, it is expected to show great analytical performances for the detection of E2 in actual serum samples. As shown in Fig. S16, the fluorescence intensity of the sensor changed as the concentration of E2 increased. As seen in Fig. S17, the linear regression equation between $\mathrm{I}_{428}/\mathrm{I}_{608}$ and the logarithm of the concentration of E2 was calculated as $\mathrm{I}_{428}/\mathrm{I}_{608}=1.46\log\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{E}2}+1.77.$ In the linear range of $0.5{-}1000~\mathrm{nM}$ , the correlation coefficient and the detection limit were found to be $\mathsf{R}^{2}=0.99184$ and $0.3~\mathrm{{nM}}$ by calculation, respectively. These satisfactory results revealed that the sensor has good prospects for practical application.
## 4. Conclusion
Sensitive and accurate detection of estrogen is essential for monitoring human endocrine levels and also for preventing illegal use of estrogen in animal husbandry to promote animal growth and development [53]. A sensitive fluorescence sensor was successfully constructed using FRET mechanism to detect the most active $17\upbeta$ -estradiol (E2) in mammalian estrogen steroids. Owing to a large overlap between the emission spectrum of the FRET donor (MIL-53-apt) and the absorption spectrum of its acceptor ${\mathrm{RuSiO}}_{2}$ -cDNA), the designed FRET sensor could achieve sensitive detection of E2. The combination of green non-toxic nanomaterials chosen and the aptamers improves the specificity of detection and the stability of the sensor [38,46,48]. Compared with previous single-signal sensors for detection of E2, the dual-signal detection improved the detection accuracy and prevented false positive results [10–15]. The sensor was applied for the detection of E2 in actual goat serum and it showed good reproducibility and selectivity, with detection limit of $0.3~\mathrm{\nM}$ . In future, we will continue to explore more applications of the ingenious combination of metal organic framework and nanomaterials for fluorescence sensing.
